---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import ancestors from "../data/ancestors.json";
const dated = ancestors.filter((a) => a.byear).sort((a, b) => a.byear - b.byear);
const oldest = dated.slice(0, 8);
const byGen = {};
for (const a of ancestors) (byGen[a.gen] ||= []).push(a);
const gens = Object.keys(byGen).map(Number).sort((a, b) => a - b);
---
<Base title="How Far Back"
  eyebrow="Nine generations, and then silence"
  dek="The honest answer is: to about 1717, in four parishes, and no further — and the reason it stops is not that the records stop."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive reaches <strong>{gens[gens.length - 1]} generations</strong> above
    Hedviga and names <strong>{ancestors.length} ancestors</strong>. The earliest with a recorded
    birth year is <strong>{oldest[0].name}, {oldest[0].byear}</strong>.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Slots possible</th><th>Named here</th><th>Filled</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{gens.map((g) => {
        const poss = Math.pow(2, g - 1), have = byGen[g].length;
        return (<tr><td>{g}</td><td class="mono">{poss}</td><td class="mono">{have}</td>
          <td class="mono">{Math.round((have / poss) * 100)}%</td></tr>);
      })}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The fall-off is the real story. Generations 1 to 4 are
    nearly complete. By generation 7 the tree is naming a small fraction of the people who must have
    existed, and by generation 9 it holds four. <strong>That is normal</strong>, and it is a
    statement about who did the research, not about who the ancestors were.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The oldest named people</h2>
    <ul class="cols">{oldest.map((p) => (
      <li><span class="mono dim">{p.byear}</span>
        <a href={u(`/people/${p.slug}/`)}>{p.name}</a>
        <span class="dim">{p.bornPlace ? ` — ${p.bornPlace}` : ""}</span></li>))}</ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Notice where they are. The deepest reach is <strong>not</strong>
    on the Blažević line — it is through <a href={u("/people/matia-matthea-pilipic/")}>Matija
    Pilipić</a>'s people at <strong>Karlobag</strong> and the Antić–Jelličić line at
    <strong>Selce</strong>. Somebody read those registers properly. Nobody has yet done the same for
    Smokvica Krmpotska.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why it stops where it stops</div>
    <h2>The records go further than the research does</h2>
    <p>The Croatian Roman Catholic parish registers digitised as
    <strong>“Croatia, Church Books, 1516–1994”</strong> begin, for some parishes, in the
    <strong>sixteenth century</strong>. The Senj births volume cited repeatedly in this tree covers
    <strong>1756–1858</strong> — and the family's use of it stops around 1838.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">So the wall at 1717 is not an archival wall. It is where the reading
    stopped. Going further needs someone who can work in <strong>Latin parish hands</strong>, who
    knows that <strong>X spells Ž</strong> in the older entries, and who is willing to
    <a href={u("/name/")}>search a name five ways</a>.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>One structural caution.</strong> Before civil registration, a parish
    register is the <em>only</em> record — and a fire, a war or a damp cellar ends a line absolutely.
    The Military Frontier was fought over repeatedly. Some of these lines will not be extendable at
    any price, and it is better to know that than to keep guessing.</div>
  </section>
</Base>
